<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">St. Georg, Nördlingen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1427–1505</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as St. Georgskirche, Nördlingen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">St. Georg is the main church of Nördlingen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, one of the largest late Gothic hall churches in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, built between 1427 and 1505, with its tower completed in 1539. The tower, about 90 metres high and known as the Daniel, overlooks the town's intact ring of medieval walls and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nordlinger-ries">Ries crater</a>, and a watchman still calls from it each evening.</p></div>
